The leptoquark $U_1$ based on the $SU(2)_L$ framework is an effective way to address various $b 	o s/c$ anomalies.  The interaction of $U_1$ results in a suppression of the process $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ compared to experimental data, suggesting new contributions from non-$U_1$ sources.  Non-$U_1$ sources that could reconcile the anomaly and explain the decay rate are explored within a leptoquark $U_1$ framework.  Constraints from $U_1$ alone are insufficient for describing both the anomaly and decay rate independently.  A specific non-$U_1$ source is found to contribute significantly to $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ while having minimal impact on other low-energy processes.  Within the combined $U_1$ + non-$U_1$ leptoquark framework, both aspects can be accounted for, with implications for $B 	o (p,\mathcal{N}) 	au  ar{
u}$ processes.
